From 30669dcbf212c579e394240242f167c5677a181a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Z User <z@container>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 01:28:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] x86/i686: string-op slot law, PC16 writer plumbing, GOTPC
 addends, @PLT/addr16/xchg.s laws
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Follow-up to the operand-direction round (PR #671 content): the Review-AI
audit's nine findings verified against live GAS 2.47.20260726 probes and
fixed, plus every sibling defect the probes surfaced along the way. All
eighty probe shapes in this message were byte-checked against the oracle
in both modes; the casefiles re-pin each law so asmdiff re-derives both
sides per run. Gates: asmdiff x86-64 1316/1316, i686 602/602, clippy
--all-targets -D warnings clean, 3857 unit tests, isa_gap_sweep
FALSEACC=0 on both architectures.

F1 (P0, confirmed) — the string-op segment scan classified by a
!has_vector_reg guess: GP movsb/movsw (the MOVSX aliases dispatched to
encode_movsx_infer_dst) stem-strip to 'movs' and took the string law, so
'movsb %es:(%edi),%eax' silently dropped 0x26 (GAS: 26 0f be 07) and
'movsb %fs:(%edi),%eax' was falsely rejected. The audit's shape rule was
right but incomplete: movs/cmps need the all-memory test, while
lods/stos/scas/ins/outs must also own the accumulator spellings
('stosb %al,(%edi)'). Both arches now classify identically.

F2 (P0, confirmed and widened) — .code16 'movsl (mem),(mem)' emitted
bare a5 (GAS: 67 66 a5) and EVERY explicit-operand string form missed
the 0x67 its 32-bit address spellings need. The ported encode_string_op
sets sized_op per operand-size choice; the pending_addr32 pre-scan is
mode-aware (16-bit registers flag in .code32, 32-bit in .code16 —
'movsb (%bx),%es:(%di)' = a4).

F3 (P0, confirmed) — the x86-64 writer plumbing for R_X86_64_PC16 was
half-wired: X86_64Arch lacked reloc_pc16()/reloc_patch_size() so local
'data16 jmp 1f' leaked a .text+2 reloc where GAS folds in place (now
byte-exact: 66 e9 c8 00, zero relocs); the jump relaxer matched the
5-byte 'cs data16 jmp .L' (2e 66 e9 rel16) as an E9 rel32 jump and
shrank it by overwriting the opcode — prefixes dropped, reloc dangling
(now excluded: an explicit data16 is a 16-bit displacement request);
the .code32-in-ELF64 translation table lacked the width-matched 8/16-bit
pairs (data16 jmp ext there errored; now maps like GAS's R_X86_64_PC16);
the rel16 fold silently truncated beyond +/-32 KiB (now errors with the
pc8-style message; GAS escalates to 66 e9 rel32 — documented residual).
Near-local data16 branches keep the requested rel16 where GAS relaxes to
'66 eb rel8' — the same fixed-rel16 policy the .code16 branches document;
far/external forms are byte-exact.

F4 (P1, confirmed) — the GOTPC finalization 'if addend == 0 { addend =
field_offset }' only held for bare _GLOBAL_OFFSET_TABLE_ uses:
'leal _GLOBAL_OFFSET_TABLE_+4(%eax),%eax' kept raw +4 (GAS: 6),
'...-2(%eax)' kept 0xfffffffe (GAS: 0), and encode_mov/encode_alu
dropped user addends entirely ('movl $GOT+2,%eax' = b8 01 where GAS
writes b8 03). The finalization is now unconditional arithmetic —
addend += field_offset — and both creators pass the parsed addend.
Byte-exact on the full matrix (lea +4/0/-2, add +2/0, mov +2/0/-3,
sub +1, the fs:-displacement form).

F5 (P1, confirmed) — the scan keyed the ES-slot on the EDI register
NAME: reversed orders mis-decided ('cmpsb (%esi),%es:(%edi)': GAS keeps
26 as a generic override, the old code dropped it), the reject
hardcoded 'operand 1' where GAS numbers the offending operand's source
position ('movsb (%esi),%fs:(%edi)' = operand 2), and two non-default
segments spliced TWO overrides onto one instruction (64 65 a4 — GAS
rejects). Replaced by a slot-indexed encode_string_op (EDI-side slot:
%es-or-none, reject before any byte push; ESI-side: keep non-%ds).

Newly pinned GAS laws beyond the audit (all byte-probed): accumulator
spellings exist for STOS/LODS only, canonical order, size-matched
register ('stosq %rax,(%rdi)' = 48 ab, 'lodsq (%rsi),%rax' = 48 ad —
all falsely rejected before); SCAS takes no register spelling; INS/OUTS
require two operands and an exact %dx port in either spelling
('insb %dx,(%rdi)' = 6c, 'insb (%eax)' rejected), INS addresses EDI and
OUTS ESI ('outsb %es:(%rsi),%dx' = 26 6e); explicit-operand
movsd/cmpsd are rejected by GAS on both readings (only bare is the
string op — i686 bare movsd was an SSE operand-count error, a5 now);
the two explicit operands must agree on address-size class, 16-bit-class
index components are rejected, 32-bit-class index components tolerated;
@PLT suffix matching is case-insensitive ('call foo@plt' promotes,
'data16 jmp foo@plt' is rejected — an i686 lowercase spelling used to
leak R_386_PC32 'ext@plt'); the i686 data16 branches never rejected
@PLT at all and the x86-64 arms pushed bytes before the reject;
xchg.s swaps the ModR/M roles and the REX assignment with them (87 ca /
49 87 c8) but leaves the accumulator short form ('xchg.s %eax,%ecx' =
91 — the audit's counter-guess probed wrong); i686 also falsely
rejected AT&T's memory-first 'xchg (%ebx),%eax' = 87 03; addr16 over a
template-forced 0x67 (monitor/umonitor 16-bit hint) is a duplicate
prefix GAS rejects ('same type of prefix used twice') — the audit's
dedupe proposal would have accepted what GAS rejects; monitor/umonitor
address prefixes are mode-dependent (.code16 'monitor %eax,...' = 67 0f
01 c8, 'umonitor %ax' = no prefix); GAS's .code16 prefix run is
FWAIT/segments/67/66/group-1-last (65 67 66 f0 01 03 — the old
interleave emitted f3 67 for 'rep movsb' with 32-bit addressing);
vmovd/vmovq vector-only spellings delegate to the shared core in 32-bit
mode (62 f1 7e 08 7e f4 / c5 fa 7e d1 — previously rejected by a stale
GP_VECTOR_MNEMONICS entry contradicting its own doc comment); the
audit's F9 casefile duplicate ('monitor %rax,%ecx,%edx' twice,
monitorx %eax,%rcx,%rdx missing) is fixed.

Gates: asmdiff x86-64 1316/1316, i686 602/602 (GAS 2.47.20260726
oracles, +4/+2 groups of new pins); clippy --all-targets -D warnings
clean; 3857 unit tests pass; isa_gap_sweep FALSEACC=0 on x86-64 and
i686.
